# Build Your First Flow

This notebook builds and runs a two-step Flow. The Flow adds one to a value,
then doubles the result. It uses the Elixir Flow module authoring surface and
returns the canonical `%Jido.Flow{}` artifact.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-rc.1"}])
```

## Define The Actions

Flow steps call normal `Jido.Action` modules. The actions validate their input
and output so the Flow can pass data between them.

```elixir
defmodule BuildFirstFlow.AddOne do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "add_one",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value}, _context), do: {:ok, %{value: value + 1}}
end

defmodule BuildFirstFlow.Double do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "double",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value}, _context), do: {:ok, %{value: value * 2}}
end
```

## Define The Flow Module

`use Jido.Flow` gives the module Flow metadata, validation callbacks, and a
`flow/0` function. A result reference creates the dependency from `"double"`
to `"add_one"`.

```elixir
defmodule BuildFirstFlow.DoubleAfterIncrement do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "double_after_increment",
    description: "Adds one, then doubles the value",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()})

  flow do
    step "add_one",
      action: BuildFirstFlow.AddOne,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    step "double",
      action: BuildFirstFlow.Double,
      params: %{value: select(result("add_one"), :value)}
  end
end
```

## Run The Flow

Use `Jido.Exec.run/3` with the Flow module. Because `double` is the only
terminal node and there is no `output`, its complete result is the Flow output.

```elixir
{:ok, %{value: 8}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(BuildFirstFlow.DoubleAfterIncrement, %{value: 3}, %{})
```

The canonical Flow artifact can also be passed directly to `Jido.Exec`.

```elixir
flow = BuildFirstFlow.DoubleAfterIncrement.flow()

%Jido.Flow{name: "double_after_increment", nodes: nodes} = flow
2 = length(nodes)

{:ok, %{value: 8}} = Jido.Exec.run(flow, %{value: 3}, %{})
```

## Inspect The Flow

The artifact contains named nodes and one canonical output expression. Use public
inspection functions to see dependencies and a deterministic explanation.

```elixir
{:ok, %{"add_one" => [], "double" => ["add_one"]}} =
  Jido.Flow.dependencies(flow)

{:ok, explanation} = Jido.Flow.explain(flow)

[%{name: "add_one"}, %{name: "double", deps: ["add_one"]}] = explanation.nodes
```

The node inputs contain data expressions. The Flow lowerer converts those
expressions into canonical references before execution.

